Frost-Dub

Creative Catalyst: Lighthouse keepers broadcasting to ships through long polar nights.

Frost-Dub is the sound of sub-zero insulation. It rejects the sunny, communal warmth of traditional Reggae, replacing it with claustrophobic, icy isolation. The signature Moog Minimoog lines are not used for funky leads but for sub-bass "earthquakes" that mimic the sound of shifting tectonic glaciers, paired with cavernous, percussive arrangements that feel like water droplets freezing on cold steel. It is a genre of solitude, where the syncopated off-beat is interpreted not as a dance, but as the rhythmic ticking of a failing life-support system.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 65–72 BPM; a heavy, lethargic pulse that drags behind the beat to mimic deep-freeze exhaustion.
*   Harmonic Palette: Aeolian and Phrygian dominant modes; minor key tonalities built on dissonance and the tension of augmented seconds.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Cryo-Bouncing:* Running tape loops through a container of dry ice to induce unpredictable pitch-warping.
       Squelch-Gating:* Using the lighthouse beacon’s literal power grid frequency as a side-chain trigger to pulse the bassline.
       Hydro-Reverb:* Capturing natural acoustics inside a hollowed-out iceberg for a wet, dense, non-reflective tail.
*   Dynamics: A flat, relentless foundation that suddenly opens into "thaw phases"—moments of blinding, high-frequency white noise that represent the lighthouse lamp turning.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the 1970s within the desolate stations of the Svalbard archipelago, Frost-Dub emerged when isolated lighthouse keepers modified their broadcast equipment to transmit low-frequency signals to passing deep-sea vessels. These men wore floor-length seal-fur trench coats and brass-goggled masks to shield their eyes from the permanent winter glare. Their ritual involved the "Broadcasting Vigil": locking themselves into the lantern room for forty-eight hours, manipulating the Minimoog while rotating the great fresnel lenses, syncing the light cycles to their sub-bass drops. To them, the bass was not music; it was a structural necessity to shatter the ice buildup on the lighthouse glass.

Instruments: Moog Minimoog (detuned), custom-built hydrophone-mics, rusted oil-drum percussion, and glass harmonica shards.

For Fans Of: Burial, Lee "Scratch" Perry’s Black Ark era, Throbbing Gristle, Scientist.

